{"page": 138, "lines": [{"t": "DSP-2232 OPERATING MANUAL", "x": 269.0, "y": 132.0}, {"t": "FACSIMILE OPERATION", "x": 1500.0, "y": 140.0}, {"t": "9.1. 2", "x": 269.0, "y": 210.0}, {"t": "9.2", "x": 262.0, "y": 849.0}, {"t": "7/92", "x": 248.0, "y": 2545.0}, {"t": "Slow Scan Television", "x": 475.0, "y": 213.0}, {"t": "Slow Scan Television (SSTV) has been around for many years and iB", "x": 475.0, "y": 293.0}, {"t": "presently found primarily in the 20 meter amateur band. Over the", "x": 474.0, "y": 334.0}, {"t": "years SSTV hag evolved a great deal and many different formats now", "x": 473.0, "y": 376.0}, {"t": "exist for both black & white as well as color image transmission.", "x": 472.0, "y": 412.0}, {"t": "SSTV uses a Frequency Modulation scheme similar to FM facsimile where", "x": 471.0, "y": 494.0}, {"t": "the video information is usually transmitted by an audio tone between", "x": 471.0, "y": 532.0}, {"t": "1,500 Hz and 2,300 Hz.", "x": 472.0, "y": 572.0}, {"t": "In SSTV however additional synchronizing", "x": 954.0, "y": 574.0}, {"t": "information is often necessary and is typically sent with tones", "x": 472.0, "y": 611.0}, {"t": "between 1 100 and 1, 500 Hz. For this reason SSTV requires a", "x": 469.0, "y": 651.0}, {"t": "different modem than is used in FM Facsimile. The DSP—2232's Modem", "x": 468.0, "y": 690.0}, {"t": "43 ig optimized for these frequencies and ig used for SSTV reception.", "x": 469.0, "y": 731.0}, {"t": "Find inq FAX Frequencies", "x": 467.0, "y": 851.0}, {"t": "The following is a list of HF FM facsimile frequencies that seem to", "x": 465.0, "y": 933.0}, {"t": "broadcast On a regular schedule. A few of these stations transmit", "x": 465.0, "y": 972.0}, {"t": "24—hours/day. We suggest you try the weather FAX frequencies listed", "x": 466.0, "y": 1012.0}, {"t": "below while you are becoming familiar with facsimile operation.", "x": 464.0, "y": 1051.0}, {"t": "After printing a few images, and you are accustomed to the sound of", "x": 463.0, "y": 1092.0}, {"t": "facsimile you will be able to tune the bands", "x": 466.0, "y": 1132.0}, {"t": "frequencies where perhaps different kinds of", "x": 465.0, "y": 1172.0}, {"t": "in search of other", "x": 1377.0, "y": 1136.0}, {"t": "pictures may be found.", "x": 1374.0, "y": 1176.0}, {"t": "weather: USB 3, 357.0 kHz", "x": 461.0, "y": 1254.0}, {"t": "Photographs :", "x": 461.0, "y": 1374.0}, {"t": "10,865.0 kHz", "x": 750.0, "y": 1296.0}, {"t": "LSB", "x": 767.0, "y": 1378.0}, {"t": "4,268.0 kHz", "x": 1049.0, "y": 1257.0}, {"t": "12,125.0 kHz", "x": 1030.0, "y": 1297.0}, {"t": "10,680.7 kHz", "x": 1030.0, "y": 1378.0}, {"t": "4,975.0 kHz", "x": 1334.0, "y": 1257.0}, {"t": "20,015.0 kHz", "x": 1314.0, "y": 1298.0}, {"t": "17,673.9 kHz", "x": 1315.0, "y": 1378.0}, {"t": "6,946.0 kHz", "x": 1623.0, "y": 1256.0}, {"t": "18,434.9 kHz", "x": 1604.0, "y": 1377.0}, {"t": "The following HF facsimile frequencies were", "x": 459.0, "y": 1495.0}, {"t": "Communications Magazine:", "x": 459.0, "y": 1536.0}, {"t": "USB", "x": 458.0, "y": 1617.0}, {"t": "obtained from Popular", "x": 1354.0, "y": 1497.0}, {"t": "4,271.", "x": 458.0, "y": 1697.0}, {"t": "O", "x": 582.0, "y": 1698.0}, {"t": "8,502.0", "x": 458.0, "y": 1737.0}, {"t": "9,389.", "x": 457.0, "y": 1777.0}, {"t": "5", "x": 583.0, "y": 1779.0}, {"t": "4,793.", "x": 457.0, "y": 1817.0}, {"t": "5", "x": 582.0, "y": 1819.0}, {"t": "9,157.", "x": 456.0, "y": 1858.0}, {"t": "5", "x": 582.0, "y": 1860.0}, {"t": "8,080.0", "x": 456.0, "y": 1899.0}, {"t": "4,802.", "x": 455.0, "y": 1940.0}, {"t": "5", "x": 581.0, "y": 1942.0}, {"t": "7,770.", "x": 455.0, "y": 1981.0}, {"t": "O", "x": 579.0, "y": 1982.0}, {"t": "8,459.", "x": 455.0, "y": 2021.0}, {"t": "O", "x": 578.0, "y": 2022.0}, {"t": "4,346.", "x": 454.0, "y": 2063.0}, {"t": "O", "x": 578.0, "y": 2063.0}, {"t": "646.0", "x": 497.0, "y": 2102.0}, {"t": "VHF and", "x": 452.0, "y": 2183.0}, {"t": "kH z", "x": 624.0, "y": 1698.0}, {"t": "kHz", "x": 623.0, "y": 1738.0}, {"t": 